To get the GNSS (Global Navigation Satellite System) timestamp on an Android device, follow these simple steps:

1. First, make sure your device has a GNSS receiver, like GPS, GLONASS, or Beidou, which are used for determining the time and location. Most smartphones have this built-in.

2. Next, you'll need to write a small program to access the GNSS data. This can be done using Android's Java API or Kotlin, depending on your preference.

3. In your code, you'll use the LocationManager class to get the GNSS data. This class provides access to location-related information, including the time the location was last updated.

4. To get the timestamp, you'll call the getLastTimeFix() method on the Location object, which returns the time when the last location was obtained. This time is usually in the form of a long value representing the number of seconds since 1/1/1970 (known as Unix time).

Here's a simple example in Java:

```
import android.location.Location;
import android.location.LocationManager;

public class GNSS_Time {
    public static void main(String args[]) {
        // Get the LocationManager service
        LocationManager locationManager = (LocationManager) getSystemService(Context.LOCATION_SERVICE);

        // Get the last GNSS timestamp
        Location location = (Location) locationManager.getLastKnownLocation(LocationManager.GPS_PROVIDER);
        long GNSS_timestamp = location.getTime();

        // Print the GNSS timestamp
        System.out.println("GNSS Timestamp: " + GNSS_timestamp);
    }
}
```

